      <h1>Victoza vs Saxenda: Same Drug, Two Very Different Jobs</h1>
      <p class="article-deck">It's one of the most searched GLP-1 comparisons, and the answer surprises people: they're the same molecule. The differences that matter are dose, licence, and where each sits now that weekly injections have taken over.</p>

      <div class="tldr-box">
        <h2>TL;DR</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Victoza and Saxenda both contain <strong>liraglutide</strong>. Chemically identical; not interchangeable in practice.</li>
          <li><strong>Victoza</strong> is licensed for type 2 diabetes, dosed up to 1.8mg daily.</li>
          <li><strong>Saxenda</strong> is licensed for weight management, dosed up to 3.0mg daily.</li>
          <li>Both are <strong>daily</strong> injections — the main reason they've been overtaken by weekly semaglutide (Ozempic/Wegovy) and tirzepatide (Mounjaro).</li>
          <li>Generic liraglutide now exists in the UK, which has made the daily option cheaper than it used to be.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <h2>One molecule, two brands</h2>
      <p>Liraglutide is a GLP-1 receptor agonist developed by Novo Nordisk — the same company behind Ozempic and Wegovy. It was first launched as <strong>Victoza</strong> in 2009 for type 2 diabetes. Trials showed patients on it reliably lost weight, so Novo ran dedicated obesity trials at a higher dose and launched the result as <strong>Saxenda</strong> in 2014 (UK availability came later).</p>
      <p>If that story sounds familiar, it should: it's exactly the playbook Novo repeated with semaglutide, where Ozempic is the diabetes brand and Wegovy is the higher-dose weight-management brand. Victoza and Saxenda were the original pair.</p>

