      <h2>Why the dose difference matters</h2>
      <p>The appetite effect of GLP-1 medications scales with dose. At Victoza's 1.8mg ceiling, liraglutide does its diabetes job — improving blood sugar control — with weight loss as a modest side benefit. Saxenda's 3.0mg dose was chosen specifically because the obesity trials showed meaningfully more weight loss at that level. Same drug; the licence follows the evidence at each dose.</p>
      <p>This is also why you can't simply take Victoza "for weight loss" and expect Saxenda results — the dose you'd be prescribed for diabetes tops out below where the weight-management evidence sits.</p>

      <h2>How they compare to the current generation</h2>
      <p>Honestly? Neither is the first choice for weight loss in 2026. In head-to-head and cross-trial comparisons, weekly semaglutide (Wegovy) roughly doubles Saxenda's average weight loss, and tirzepatide (Mounjaro) goes further still. Add the convenience gap — one injection a week versus one every day — and it's clear why prescribing has shifted decisively to the weekly options. See our full <a href="/uk-glp1-price-comparison">UK price comparison</a> for what each currently costs.</p>
      <p>Where daily liraglutide still earns a place:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Tolerance.</strong> Some people who can't tolerate semaglutide or tirzepatide side effects do better on liraglutide. A shorter-acting daily drug also clears the system faster if you stop.</li>
        <li><strong>Price.</strong> With generic liraglutide now on the UK market, the daily option can undercut the branded weeklies for some buyers.</li>
        <li><strong>Supply.</strong> During the shortage years, liraglutide was sometimes the option that was actually in stock. Supply has largely normalised, but it remains a fallback.</li>
        <li><strong>Clinical history.</strong> Liraglutide has been in wide use since 2009 — the long-term safety record is the deepest of any GLP-1.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Name check:</strong> searching for "liraglutide brand name"? It's marketed as Victoza (diabetes) and Saxenda (weight management), plus generic versions simply sold as liraglutide. Rybelsus, which sometimes gets mixed into this comparison, is different — that's <em>oral semaglutide</em>, a tablet, not liraglutide at all.
      </div>

      <h2>Side effects: essentially identical</h2>
      <p>Because it's the same molecule, the side-effect profile is shared: nausea, constipation or diarrhoea, and injection-site reactions are the common ones, usually worst during dose escalation. Saxenda users report gastrointestinal effects somewhat more often, which tracks with the higher dose. The serious-but-rare warnings (pancreatitis, gallbladder issues) apply to both, as they do across the GLP-1 class. Our guide to <a href="/managing-glp1-nausea">managing GLP-1 nausea</a> applies equally here.</p>

      <h2>The bottom line</h2>
      <p>Victoza vs Saxenda isn't really a "which is better" question — it's the same drug wearing two uniforms. Victoza (1.8mg) is the diabetes formulation; Saxenda (3.0mg) is the weight-management one. The more useful 2026 question is whether daily liraglutide in any form makes sense for you compared with weekly semaglutide or tirzepatide — and for most people starting fresh, it's the weeklies, with liraglutide as a well-understood, increasingly cheap plan B.</p>

